; Timing annotations for usb_enum.pasm at FULL speed (48 MHz, TURN = 4), kept
; beside the source so the firmware file stays untouched (docs/timing-check.md):
;   python -m pe.timing firmware/usb_enum.pasm --timing firmware/usb_enum.timing -D DP=0 -D DM=1 -D TURN=4
; Same conventions as usb_fs_dev.pasm: the core sees the host's J 2..6 ticks after
; the frame end reaches the firmware (tok_end and d_end are 2 ticks after the
; `in line` that returned T = 1); pads: 3..4 clocks; the line transmitter is idle
; when a response starts (stall=...:0).
; The firmware waits for the J before each response with a timed WAIT (in_tmo,
; hs_tmo: 121 ticks), so that a bus reset that runs on from the host's EOP gets
; out to `se0` (docs/fault-testing.md, finding 6). The checker's `.cond` takes
; an untimed WAIT only, so the turnaround is timed from the frame end instead:
; the WAIT is reached 7 ticks or more after it, after the J (2..6), and so
; completes at its first attempt (stall=in_tmo:0 / hs_tmo:0: in_reach and
; hs_reach below prove the 7 ticks). From the J the response then takes the interval less
; 2..6 ticks, plus the pads' 3..4: adjust=-3..2. (Until fault-fixes these were
; `.cond` deadlines on an untimed `wait 1 pin DM`, with the same bounds.)
@point in_tmo at=got_in+1             ; wait 1 pin DM timeout r0 (r0 = 120)
@point in_out at=got_in+4             ; out line, 16: SYNC + DATA0/DATA1/STALL
@point hs_tmo at=handshake+2          ; wait 1 pin DM timeout r0 (r0 = 120)
@point hs_out at=handshake+4          ; out line, 16: SYNC + ACK/STALL
@point not_in at=ours+4               ; cmp r0, 0x2D: the token is not an IN (no answer to time)
@point lost_ack at=tok_end+6          ; mov r3, r2: the inline commit of a token to the new address, status ACK lost
; in_ta excludes `lost_ack` (the usual case: the status ACK arrived, or the
; first token to the new address is a SETUP) and passes conditionally. in_ta_any
; is the required one: it includes `lost_ack`, and every path from a token's end
; either answers an IN or is excluded by name (`idle`: not for us; `not_in`: a
; SETUP, OUT or other PID, which gets no answer), so a plain PASS. Until enum-ta
; the lost_ack path went through `call commit` and `prep` and FAILED (an IN
; answered up to 30 clocks after the host's EOP; 27.4-28.2 on the model); the
; commit is now inline, without `prep`, and the path is 6 ticks longer than in_ta.
@deadline in_ta from=tok_end.enter to=in_out avoid=idle,got_setup,expect,lost_ack stall=in_tmo:0,in_out:0 min=8 max=26 adjust=-3..2 reach=conditional "IN token -> DATA/STALL (r1 prepared ahead)"
@deadline in_ta_any from=tok_end.enter to=in_out avoid=idle,not_in stall=in_tmo:0,in_out:0 min=8 max=26 adjust=-3..2 "the same, with the lost-ACK commit path"
@deadline data_ta from=d_end.enter to=hs_out avoid=idle stall=hs_tmo:0,hs_out:0 min=8 max=26 adjust=-3..2 "DATA0/1 -> ACK/STALL"
@deadline rx_bit from=d_bit to=d_bit.enter avoid=d_end max=4 reach=conditional "DATA receive loop: a bit every 4 clocks"
; The stall=in_tmo:0 / hs_tmo:0 above, from the program itself: the timed
; WAITs are first reached 7 ticks or more after the frame end, so after the J
; (2..6) whatever the outside world does. (Report 023 found the soundness runs
; only counted these assumptions; the checker now proves them.)
@deadline in_reach from=tok_end.enter to=in_tmo.enter avoid=idle,not_in min=7 unit=tick "frame end -> got_in's J WAIT: after the J"
@deadline hs_reach from=d_end.enter to=hs_tmo.enter avoid=idle min=7 unit=tick "frame end -> handshake's J WAIT: after the J"
